TDLib UPGRADE — PHASE 3 SOURCE MIGRATION (GATED — DO NOT APPLY UNTIL THE NEW VSIX IS BUILT)
==========================================================================================

These changes are required ONLY when you upgrade to a TDLib build that uses the
new schema (TDLib >= 1.8.6), where the `tdlibParameters` object was removed and
its fields were merged directly into `setTdlibParameters`, and
`checkDatabaseEncryptionKey` was removed (the encryption key is now a field on
`setTdlibParameters`).

Applying these BEFORE the matching Telegram.Td.UWP VSIX is installed will break
the current build, because the current VSIX (TDLib ~1.7.x) still exposes the old
`TdlibParameters` type.

Verify against the EXACT TDLib version you built: open the generated
Telegram.Td.Api namespace and check whether `TdlibParameters` and
`CheckDatabaseEncryptionKey` still exist. If they do, KEEP the current code.


1) ProtoService.Initialize()  — Unigram/Unigram/Services/ProtoService.cs
------------------------------------------------------------------------

CURRENT (old schema, ~line 214 and ~line 334):

    var parameters = new TdlibParameters
    {
        DatabaseDirectory = ...,
        UseSecretChats = true,
        UseMessageDatabase = true,
        ApiId = Constants.ApiId,
        ApiHash = Constants.ApiHash,
        ApplicationVersion = ...,
        SystemVersion = ...,
        SystemLanguageCode = ...,
        DeviceModel = ...,
        UseTestDc = _settings.UseTestDC
    };
    if (_settings.FilesDirectory != null) { parameters.FilesDirectory = _settings.FilesDirectory; }
    ...
    _client.Send(new SetTdlibParameters(parameters));
    _client.Send(new CheckDatabaseEncryptionKey(new byte[0]));

NEW schema (TDLib >= 1.8.6) — the fields move onto SetTdlibParameters and the
database encryption key becomes the first constructor argument / DatabaseEncryptionKey
field. Remove the CheckDatabaseEncryptionKey call entirely:

    var parameters = new SetTdlibParameters
    {
        DatabaseEncryptionKey = new byte[0],
        DatabaseDirectory = System.IO.Path.Combine(ApplicationData.Current.LocalFolder.Path, $"{_session}"),
        UseSecretChats = true,
        UseMessageDatabase = true,
        ApiId = Constants.ApiId,
        ApiHash = Constants.ApiHash,
        ApplicationVersion = _deviceInfoService.ApplicationVersion,
        SystemVersion = _deviceInfoService.SystemVersion,
        SystemLanguageCode = _deviceInfoService.SystemLanguageCode,
        DeviceModel = _deviceInfoService.DeviceModel,
        UseTestDc = _settings.UseTestDC
    };
    if (_settings.FilesDirectory != null) { parameters.FilesDirectory = _settings.FilesDirectory; }
    ...
    _client.Send(parameters);
    // CheckDatabaseEncryptionKey removed — key is now part of SetTdlibParameters.

NOTE: exact field names (e.g. UseTestDc vs UseTestDC, FilesDirectory) follow the
generated C# casing of the version you build — let IntelliSense confirm them.


2) #if MOCKUP positional constructors — ProtoService.cs (~line 233 onward)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

The MOCKUP block builds User(...), Supergroup(...), Chat(...), Message(...),
ProfilePhoto(...), ChatPhoto(...) etc. using POSITIONAL constructors. TDLib's
generated constructors change parameter order/count between versions, so the
MOCKUP block will fail to compile after an upgrade.

Action: rebuild the MOCKUP block against the new constructors, or switch to
object-initializer syntax (new User { Id = ..., FirstName = ... }) which is
resilient to added fields. MOCKUP is a design-time-only path (#if MOCKUP), so it
does not affect release builds — fix it last, or temporarily exclude it.


3) ID type widening audit — NotificationsService.ProcessAsync and callers
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

In the current (~1.7.x) headers, chat/user/supergroup IDs are already `long`,
but secret-chat and sticker IDs are still `int`. Newer TDLib widens more IDs to
`long`. Audit for `int.TryParse(...)` / `(int)` casts on IDs and for
CreatePrivateChat(fromId, ...) style calls, and widen locals to `long` as the
compiler flags them.


4) Recommended verification order
---------------------------------
  a. Build the new VSIX with Build-TdLib.ps1 and install it locally.
  b. Apply change (1). Build for x86 (Debug) against the local machine /
     emulator first — fastest iteration.
  c. Fix compiler errors surfaced by (3) as they appear.
  d. Fix the MOCKUP block (2) or exclude it until the end.
  e. Once x86 builds and runs, build ARM Release for the device.
  f. Smoke-test: login, receive messages, and confirm the background refresh
     (Phase 1) still delivers toasts.
